<comment datatype='string'>The &lt;a href=&quot;/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=G-12258&quot;&gt;paaA&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=G-12259&quot;&gt;paaB&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=G-12260&quot;&gt;paaC&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href=&quot;/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=G-12261&quot;&gt;paaE&lt;/a&gt; genes of &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-198931&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Pseudomonas&lt;/i&gt; sp. &lt;i&gt;Y2&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt; were cloned and expressed simultaneously in &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-562&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Escherichia coli&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt;. PaaA alone was cloned as a maltose-binding protein (MBD) fusion protein. When purified by amylose resin chromatography, all proteins co-purified [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/20660314&quot;&gt;Teufel10&lt;/a&gt;].
&lt;p&gt;
The purified PaaABCE complex catalyzed a &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CPD-207&quot;&gt;phenylacetyl-CoA&lt;/a&gt;-induced and strictly oxygen-dependent NADPH consumption in a spectrophotometric assay, implying phenylacetyl-CoA oxygenase/reductase activity. The product was purified using reverse phase (RP)-HPLC, and mass spectrometry showed the incorporation of a single oxygen atom. &lt;sup&gt;18&lt;/sup&gt;O studies showed that the oxygen atom originates from molecular oxygen, and &lt;sup&gt;13&lt;/sup&gt;C-NMR analysis suggested that the product is &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CPD0-2362&quot;&gt;2-(1,2-epoxy-1,2-dihydrophenyl)acetyl-CoA&lt;/a&gt;. Further proof was provided by derivatization of the epoxide.
Thus it was concluded that the PaaABCE complex functions as a &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=ENZYME&amp;object=CPLX-8168&quot;&gt;ring 1,2-phenylacetyl-CoA epoxidase&lt;/a&gt; [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/20660314&quot;&gt;Teufel10&lt;/a&gt;].</comment>
